      "text_raw": "The transconductance g is\nm\nnow easily calculated, as it\nis the derivative of the cur-\nrent. Instead the ratio\ng /I is taken and normal-\nm DS\nized to nkT/q. It is denoted\nby GM. The approxim-\nations for strong inversion (i\n>10) and weak inversion (i\n<0.1) are easily obtained.\nThe full expression is\nplotted in the next slide.\nSometimes an alternative\nfunction is used to describe\nthe transition region. It is a\nlittle closer to measured\ndata. It involves square roots however, instead of exponentials. For strong inversion, it provides\nthe same approximation. However, for weak inversion, it is slightly smaller.\nBoth functions are plotted in the next slide.",
      "text": "The transconductance g is m now easily calculated, as it is the derivative of the current. Instead the ratio g /I is taken and normalm DS ized to nkT/q. It is denoted by GM. The approximations for strong inversion (i >10) and weak inversion (i <0.1) are easily obtained. The full expression is plotted in the next slide. Sometimes an alternative function is used to describe the transition region. It is a little closer to measured data. It involves square roots however, instead of exponentials. For strong inversion, it provides the same approximation. However, for weak inversion, it is slightly smaller. Both functions are plotted in the next slide."
    }
